    "product_id": "e9320622-50af-4e75-9bc7-7fd17dcc5254",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 090916\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n115 AM PST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Quiet and pleasant conditions anticipated through Friday.\n\n* Models continue to suggest the return of an active pattern this\n  weekend and into next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through the weekend.\n\nUpper-level ridging generally remains in place through Thursday, \namplifying after the passage of a weak disturbance on Tuesday. At \nthe surface, temperatures are forecast to be 2-6 degrees above \nnormal while dry conditions prevail. Winds largely stay on the light \nside, although breezy north winds are anticipated in the lower \nColorado River Valley on Wednesday in the wake of the \naforementioned disturbance.\n\nBy Friday, the ridge axis begins to shift east as a closed low \napproaches the SoCal/Baja coast. Over the last 24 hours, both \nphysics-based and AI guidance has trended more progressive and \nfarther south with this feature. As a result, PoPs have fallen below \n20% on Saturday across our southern zones. This track shift also \nleads to even less of a response in temperatures and winds. As for \nthe larger trough in the northeast Pacific, there is not much run-to-\nrun nor inter-model consistency in terms of timing and evolution. \nLatest ECMWF guidance shows a large shift compared to just 12 hours \nago, now having a lead shortwave swing through the PacNW/Great Basin \non Saturday and attempting to phase with the southern system, with \nthe main trough axis arriving early next week. Meanwhile, the GFS \n(ensemble and deterministic) have the lead shortwave farther north \nand the main trough coming through Sunday into Monday as a weaker \nsystem. Needless to say, forecast uncertainty grows substantially by \nthe weekend. Best chances for precipitation, cooler temperatures, \nand increased winds appears to be late this weekend and into early \nnext week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...\nLight winds continue for the entire TAF period with diurnal \ndirectional changes expected. Light WSW flow overnight becomes light \nand variable toward sunrise. Winds then become northeasterly around \nand after 19z but remaining light under 8 kt. Expect increasing high \nclouds tomorrow with bases at or above 20 kft. VFR conditions will \npersist through the TAF period.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Generally light winds \nare favored across the regional TAF sites with winds favoring typical\ndaily directional trends. Exception will be near KIFP where \nnortherly breezes will persist, though much lighter than the past \nfew days. Mostly clear skies are expected region-wide through Monday \nmorning before increasing high clouds move through Monday afternoon \nand evening. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Moore/CO\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
